{"uid":"19bb59c0c11e310b","name":"test_create_customer_fail_for_existing_email","fullName":"tests.customers.api.test_create_customer#test_create_customer_fail_for_existing_email","historyId":"28ba8e25500f879aaa38b2747a0955ec","time":{"start":1792351748145,"stop":1792351748604,"duration":459},"description":"\nVerify that customer creation rejects an email address that already exists.\n\nThis test intentionally combines two different test-data flows:\n\n1. Valid test-data setup:\n   create_valid_customer()\n       ↓\n   CustomerBuilder\n       ↓\n   CustomerFactory\n       ↓\n   CustomerProvisioner\n       ↓\n   CustomerHelper / CustomersApi\n       ↓\n   WooCommerce\n\n   The fixture creates the original customer and registers it for cleanup.\n\n2. Negative operation under test:\n   Test\n     ↓\n   Raw API Client\n     ↓\n   POST /customers with the existing email\n     ↓\n   WooCommerce\n     ↓\n   HTTP 400\n\nThe duplicate-email payload is intentionally constructed inside the test\nbecause it represents the invalid scenario being tested. It must not go\nthrough the valid-data Factory/Builder pipeline.\n\nTest flow:\n    1. Create a valid customer through the test-data fixture.\n    2. Extract the generated email from the created customer.\n    3. Attempt to create another customer using the same email.\n    4. Verify that WooCommerce returns HTTP 400.\n    5. Validate the duplicate-email error response.\n    6. Verify that the original customer still exists in the API.\n    7. Verify that the original customer still matches the database record.\n    8. Validate the error response against the error contract schema.\n\nCleanup:\n    The original customer is owned by this test and is automatically\n    registered for cleanup by create_valid_customer().\n","descriptionHtml":"<p>Verify that customer creation rejects an email address that already exists.</p>\n<p>This test intentionally combines two different test-data flows:</p>\n<ol>\n<li>\n<p>Valid test-data setup:<br />\ncreate_valid_customer()<br />\n↓<br />\nCustomerBuilder<br />\n↓<br />\nCustomerFactory<br />\n↓<br />\nCustomerProvisioner<br />\n↓<br />\nCustomerHelper / CustomersApi<br />\n↓<br />\nWooCommerce</p>\n<p>The fixture creates the original customer and registers it for cleanup.</p>\n</li>\n<li>\n<p>Negative operation under test:<br />\nTest<br />\n↓<br />\nRaw API Client<br />\n↓<br />\nPOST /customers with the existing email<br />\n↓<br />\nWooCommerce<br />\n↓<br />\nHTTP 400</p>\n</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The duplicate-email payload is intentionally constructed inside the test<br />\nbecause it represents the invalid scenario being tested. It must not go<br />\nthrough the valid-data Factory/Builder pipeline.</p>\n<p>Test flow:<br />\n1. Create a valid customer through the test-data fixture.<br />\n2. Extract the generated email from the created customer.<br />\n3. Attempt to create another customer using the same email.<br />\n4. Verify that WooCommerce returns HTTP 400.<br />\n5. Validate the duplicate-email error response.<br />\n6. Verify that the original customer still exists in the API.<br />\n7. Verify that the original customer still matches the database record.<br />\n8. Validate the error response against the error contract schema.</p>\n<p>Cleanup:<br />\nThe original customer is owned by this test and is automatically<br />\nregistered for cleanup by create_valid_customer().</p>\n","status":"passed","flaky":false,"newFailed":false,"newBroken":false,"newPassed":false,"retriesCount":0,"retriesStatusChange":false,"beforeStages":[{"name":"_session_faker","time":{"start":1792351742313,"stop":1792351742363,"duration":50},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage":false,"stepsCount":0,"attachmentsCount":0,"hasContent":false,"attachmentStep":false},{"name":"delete_output_dir","time":{"start":1792351742363,"stop":1792351742363,"duration":0},"status":"passed","steps":[],"attachments":[],"parameters":[],"shouldDisplayMessage"